<li id="ref20"><a href="https://www.cochrane.org/evidence/CD014789_stimulasi-sumsum-tulang-belakang-untuk-nyeri-punggung-bawah">Cochrane SCS low-back-pain review, 2023</a>. Supports not scaling SCS for nonspecific chronic low back pain.</li>
<li id="ref21"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29198932/">Vickers et al., Journal of Pain 2018</a>. Supports small but persistent acupuncture effects in several chronic pain conditions.</li>
<li id="ref22"><a href="https://www.bmj.com/content/364/bmj.l689">Rubinstein et al., BMJ 2019</a>. Supports small effects of spinal manipulative therapy for chronic low back pain.</li>
<li id="ref23"><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6242300/">Hu et al., dry needling review 2018</a>. Supports short-term dry needling signals and weak durability evidence.</li>
<li id="ref24"><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6661560/">Herd et al., Cochrane botulinum toxin migraine review</a>. Supports botulinum toxin A for chronic migraine with modest mean headache-day reduction.</li>
<li id="ref25"><a href="https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ta260/chapter/1-guidance">NICE TA260</a>. Supports coverage criteria for botulinum toxin in chronic migraine.</li>
<li id="ref26"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39476339/">Bliddal et al., NEJM 2024</a>. Supports semaglutide 2.4 mg improving weight and WOMAC pain in obesity-linked knee OA.</li>
<li id="ref27"><a href="https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/2601490">Cherkin et al., JAMA follow-up</a>. Supports MBSR and CBT improving chronic low-back-pain function and bothersomeness versus usual care.</li>
<li id="ref28"><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5368208/">Hilton et al., mindfulness chronic pain review 2017</a>. Supports modest and low-certainty mindfulness benefits across chronic pain.</li>
<li id="ref29"><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7728933/">Mackey et al., opioid dose reduction rapid review 2020</a>. Supports caution about tapering evidence and harms reporting.</li>
<li id="ref30"><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3208163/">Hill et al., STarT Back trial 2011</a>. Supports stratified low-back-pain care in the original UK implementation.</li>
<li id="ref31"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29790073/">Cherkin et al., MATCH trial 2018</a>. Supports the weaker/null US implementation of risk-stratified low-back-pain care.</li>
<li id="ref32"><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8518659/">Danish stratified low-back-pain trial</a>. Supports reduced resource use without clear superior patient outcomes.</li>
<li id="ref33"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17204567/">Christensen et al., weight reduction knee OA meta-analysis</a>. Supports dose-related pain/function benefit from substantial weight loss in knee OA.</li>
<li id="ref34"><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5876887/">Darnall et al., patient-centered opioid tapering 2018</a>. Supports voluntary tapering feasibility without mean pain worsening in a selected cohort.</li>
<li id="ref35"><a href="https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamanetworkopen/fullarticle/2784021">Powell et al., JAMA Network Open 2021</a>. Supports low-quality but plausible evidence for buprenorphine rotation in chronic pain on long-term opioids.</li>
<li id="ref36"><a href="https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/71/rr/rr7103a1.htm">CDC opioid prescribing guideline, 2022</a>. Supports collaborative opioid risk-benefit review and warnings against abrupt discontinuation.</li>
<li id="ref37"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33740342/">Xu et al., peripheral nerve stimulation review 2021</a>. Supports condition-specific and still-limited PNS evidence.</li>
<li id="ref38"><a href="https://www.bmj.com/content/374/bmj.n1034">Wang et al., BMJ cannabinoids chronic pain review 2021</a>. Supports small average benefits and increased adverse events.</li>
<li id="ref39"><a href="https://www.bmj.com/content/374/bmj.n2040">BMJ Rapid Recommendation, 2021</a>. Supports only a weak recommendation for non-inhaled cannabis/cannabinoids after standard care is insufficient.</li>
</ol>

